<!--
  ============================================================================
  JawaLeaderTitles.xml              Jawa_Patches - new file, 2026-08-22
  ============================================================================

  LEADER_TITLES_ON_THE_IDEO_1. DECIDE ruled all twelve authored factions must
  show their AUTHORED leader title. Measured live 2026-08-22,
  `jawa/faction_leader_get`: the ideoligion overrode the def on 36 of 37
  factions. The Junkers' Scraplord read `Awoken Cheese`; Blackstar's Captain
  read `Ethical Thug`.

  🔑 THE MECHANISM, READ FROM SOURCE RATHER THAN GUESSED
  =====================================================
  `Faction.LeaderTitle` (RimWorld/Faction.cs:142) prefers
  `ideos.PrimaryIdeo.leaderTitleMale` and only falls back to `def.leaderTitle`
  when that is empty. So the FactionDef field is correct, present, and unread.

  The ideo's title is written ONCE by `IdeoFoundation.GenerateLeaderTitle()`
  (RimWorld/IdeoFoundation.cs:695):

      if (ideo.culture.leaderTitleMaker == null) { leaderTitleMale = null; }
      else NameGenerator.GenerateName(request, rootKeyword: "r_leaderTitle")

  ⇒ the title comes from a `RulePackDef` hanging off the ideo's **CultureDef**.
  ⭐ That is a THIRD route, and better than either the item proposed. A precept
  cannot supply it, and a post-worldgen bridge pass would have to run in the
  same session as world creation and then save - fragile, and unavailable to
  anyone re-rolling. This is declarative, and it bakes correctly because it
  runs DURING ideo generation.

  Vanilla's own titles are assembled from `LeaderTitleMaker_GlobalStructure`,
  which offers four `r_leaderTitle` rules at p=6/3/1/1 combining meme and
  culture adjectives and nouns. Each rulepack below deliberately does NOT
  include that structure and declares exactly ONE rule at **p=1000**, so the
  authored string wins outright. ⚠️ `GenerateLeaderTitle` also folds in every
  meme's `generalRules` via `IncludesBare`; no Core meme defines
  `r_leaderTitle`, but the priority makes that irrelevant.

  ⚠️ WHY A WHOLE CultureDef PER FACTION AND NOT A PATCH ON THE SHARED ONE.
  `leaderTitleMaker` lives on the CultureDef, and six of our twelve share
  `Rustican` with every outlander faction in 578 mods. Patching it would
  rename the leader of all of them. So each faction gets its own culture, with
  every other field COPIED FROM ITS BASE - the makers, the style categories and
  the style item tags are byte-identical, so pawn appearance and deity naming
  are unchanged. Copied programmatically out of the live 578-mod dump, not
  retyped.

  ⛔ `allowedCultures` is a LIST, so a bare child node APPENDS and the game
  would pick between the base culture and ours at random. Every FactionDef edit
  that goes with this file therefore carries `Inherit="False"` or is a Replace
  on a def that writes its own node.

  ⚠️ `iconPath` points at OUR OWN faction markers, not at the vanilla path the
  base culture used. Two reasons: the culture icon then matches the faction it
  belongs to, and Core ships NO loose `Textures/` folder - its art lives in
  asset bundles - so `validate_patch.py` reports every vanilla texture path as
  missing. That is the known blind spot `reading-rimworld-graphics` exists for;
  using our own PNGs keeps this file provably green instead of permanently red.

  ⚠️ THIS BAKES. An Ideo is generated once at world creation and cannot be
  retrofitted, so it must be in place before the owner's worldgen click.
  ============================================================================
-->
<Defs>

  <!-- Jawa Trade Moot -> "Prime Trader" -->
  <RulePackDef>
    <defName>Jawa_LeaderTitle_TradeMoot</defName>
    <rulePack>
      <rulesStrings>
        <li>r_leaderTitle(p=1000)->Prime Trader</li>
      </rulesStrings>
    </rulePack>
  </RulePackDef>

